<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.15.4: Connecting the Common Ratio and the Term in a Sequence</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01128</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.15.4: Connecting the Common Ratio and the Term in a Sequence</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e0feeebe-1f57-4b74-933d-98db0250b21b</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Here is a sequence: 500, 100, 20, . . .</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    <para>Explain why this sequence could be a geometric sequence.</para>
  </item>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> The given terms have a common ratio. \(100=\frac15\cdot 500\), and \(20=\frac15\cdot 100\).</para>
  <item>
    <para>What is the next term?</para>
  </item>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> \(20 \cdot \frac15 = 4\)</para>
  <item>
    <para>Explain how you know the next term.</para>
  </item>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> Multiplying a term by the common ratio gives the next term.</para>
</list>
<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title> More Chances</title>
<para>Students will have more opportunities to understand the mathematical ideas in this cool-down, so there is no need to slow down or add additional work to the next lessons. Instead, use the results of this cool-down to provide guidance for what to look for and emphasize over the next several lessons to support students in advancing their current understanding.</para></section>
</content>
</document>
